## 2. Setting Up Secure AWS Access

Before connecting Python to AWS, I needed to configure secure authentication.

I used **AWS IAM Identity Center** instead of creating long-lived IAM access keys.

### My configuration

| Component | Configuration |
|---|---|
| AWS Region | eu-west-2 (London) |
| Authentication | IAM Identity Center (SSO) |
| Engineering user | focusgroup-engineer |
| Permission set | FocusGuard-Identity-Inspector |
| AWS CLI profile | focusguard-dev |
| SSO session | focusgroup |
| Operating system | macOS |
| Programming language | Python |

### Why use IAM Identity Center?

IAM Identity Center allows me to authenticate using a central sign-in process and obtain temporary AWS credentials.

This is preferable to embedding permanent AWS access keys inside Python scripts.

It also supports assigning specific permissions to users through permission sets.

For this project, I configured a limited engineering role rather than relying on root or administrator access.

